   * They were flex items on `align-items: center`, and 「含 PM10（有缺陷的做法）」
   * wraps while 「不含 PM10」 does not — so measured at 1000 the cards were 256px
   * and 220px tall with tops 18px apart, and 0.772 and 0.524, the two numbers a
   * reader is asked to compare, sat on different lines.
   *
   * `subgrid` is the right tool for this in principle and was tried twice. It
   * does not take here — measured, the card's rows still resolved to four fixed
   * sizes and the values stayed 25 to 49px apart at 500, 640, 768 and 1000,
   * every width where the two tags wrap to different line counts. A rule that
   * looks correct and does nothing is worse than a cruder one that is checkable.
   *
   * `align-items: stretch` makes the cards the same height; `margin-top: auto`
   * on the value pushes it, the label and the note — three single lines in both
   * cards — to a shared bottom, whatever the tag above them did.
   */
  /*
   * One grid for both cards, so the rows line up without being padded.
   *
   * The two cards differ in two places and not the same one each time: in block
   * 02 the TAG wraps (「含 PM10（有缺陷的做法）」 against 「不含 PM10」) and in
   * block 07 the NOTE does (「只答 61%，其餘拒答」 against 「答了 70%」). The
   * previous answer pinned both — `min-height: 2lh` on every tag and
   * `margin-top: auto` on every note — which aligned them by making every card
   * as tall as its worst case. Measured at 1832, where neither tag wraps: a
   * 495 x 244px card whose four lines of content inked **11%** of it, a blank
   * line reserved under each tag, and the note pushed to a bottom it had no
   * reason to be at.
   *
   * `subgrid` does what pinning was standing in for: the parent owns the three
   * rows, both cards span them, and each row is as tall as the taller of the
   * two cards' content — one line when neither wraps, two when one does. The
   * comment this replaces recorded that subgrid 「does not take here」; it does,
   * with `grid-row: span 3` on the card, which is what the earlier attempt was
   * missing.
   *
   * The value and its unit share row two, because that is how the pair is
   * written down anywhere else: 「2.88 μg/m³ 平均絕對誤差」, not the number and
   * its unit on separate lines with the width beside them unused.
   */
  .compare {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
    align-items: start;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0;
  }

  .compare .card {
    display: grid;
    grid-row: span 3;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.3rem 0.5rem;
  }

  .compare .tag,
  .compare .stat-note {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .compare .stat-value {
    grid-column: 1;
  }

  .compare .stat-label {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .compare .arrow {
    grid-row: span 3;
    align-self: center;
  }

  /*
   * Stacked, the arrow points at nothing.
   *
   * It reads as 「this became that」 across a row; down a column the same glyph
   * is just a mark between two boxes. It was hidden at every width by accident
   * — `ffd49d7` added this rule without the `@media` line that was meant to
   * wrap it, plus the stray brace that went with it, so a 「→」 and a 「vs」 the
   * markup still carries have been `display: none` since 2026-08-01.
   */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .compare {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .compare .arrow {
      display: none;
    }
  }

